WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.200
Marvel's Red Guardian is essentially&nbsp;
the Russian version of Captain America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:11.440
and this Soviet agent has always been a morally&nbsp;
complicated character. So let's take a deep dive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:16.240
into the world of Russian superheroes and&nbsp;
discover the untold truth of Red Guardian.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:17.998
"Ha ha ha! Still fits!"

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:24.240
The creation of Alexei Shostakov, aka&nbsp;
Red Guardian, or someone like him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:30.240
was kind of inevitable. The Cold War was a&nbsp;
big part of 1960s Marvel Comics storytelling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:35.520
and having a Soviet doppelganger was&nbsp;
practically required for the major superheroes.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:40.640
The Hulk fought the Abomination, a KGB agent&nbsp;
who'd been changed by gamma rays into an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:47.280
even bigger and uglier green monster. Iron Man&nbsp;
faced the Crimson Dynamo, a Soviet physicist who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:52.800
built his own suit of super-armor. Spider-Man&nbsp;
even went up against what seemed to be an exact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:58.080
duplicate of himself, but it turned out to be a&nbsp;
Russian super-agent better known as the Chameleon.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:01.120
Interestingly, Steve Rogers'&nbsp;
counterpart first appeared not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:05.760
in a Captain America comic but&nbsp;
in Avengers #43. Stranger still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:10.320
the first time Cap and the team encountered&nbsp;
Shostakov, they weren't in Russia. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:15.200
they found Shostakov in China, where he was&nbsp;
working with a Chinese agent named Colonel Ling.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:19.360
Captain America immediately recognized&nbsp;
the Red Guardian as a copy of himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:23.600
which Shostakov acknowledged. Instead&nbsp;
of a shield, this Red Guardian had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:28.240
detachable belt buckle that he could throw&nbsp;
as a weapon and then recall using magnets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:32.800
It was a pretty silly 1960s gimmick, but the&nbsp;
important thing was that having something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:37.920
round to throw at his enemies gave him that&nbsp;
recognizable Captain America-esque aesthetic.

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:42.880
Although she wouldn't officially join the&nbsp;
Avengers until a few years later, the Black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:48.240
Widow was with Earth's Mightiest Heroes when they&nbsp;
first met the Red Guardian. By this time, Widow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:54.160
had stopped being a KGB spy, and she was a close&nbsp;
ally and love interest of the Avenger Hawkeye.

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:56.160
But as soon as she saw the Red Guardian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:00.720
Natasha knew she recognized him, but she&nbsp;
couldn't believe who he actually was.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:05.280
It turned out that the husband she'd long&nbsp;
thought was dead had been alive all along.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:09.600
Alexei Shostakov's death was faked by&nbsp;
the KGB so that he could be secretly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:14.000
trained as the Red Guardian. Even though&nbsp;
he'd allowed his wife to think he'd died,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.840
he was embittered when he learned she'd&nbsp;
defected to the U.S. and was fraternizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:23.920
with American superheroes. Natasha remembered&nbsp;
her husband as a great Soviet pilot and war hero,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:27.280
but she was unprepared for the&nbsp;
costumed super-brute he'd become.

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:32.480
It's easy to imagine a version of the Red&nbsp;
Guardian who stuck around and became one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:37.280
of Captain America's top villains. He could've&nbsp;
antagonized Cap again and again, with a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:42.480
scheme each time, like a Cold War equivalent&nbsp;
of Captain America's original archenemy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:47.360
the Nazi Red Skull. If Stan Lee had still been&nbsp;
plotting the Avengers when he was introduced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:52.320
that might've happened. By 1967, however, a&nbsp;
younger generation of writers was beginning to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:57.840
take over at Marvel, and Red Guardian co-creator&nbsp;
Roy Thomas took things in a different direction.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:00.720
“Don’t make me come down there, you punk!”

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:05.280
The Red Guardian wasn't a nice guy, and&nbsp;
you couldn't call him a superhero. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:10.640
he had a certain nobility to him. After all,&nbsp;
how else could he be the first love of someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:15.520
like Black Widow, who ultimately turned out&nbsp;
to be a hero herself? He also had a measure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:20.080
of respect for Captain America, the hero&nbsp;
who'd inspired his new costumed persona.

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:24.560
So after running into the Avengers in China,&nbsp;
Shostakov pulled a pretty impressive move,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:30.000
sacrificing himself for Black Widow and Captain&nbsp;
America. The superheroes were about to escape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.680
amid the complete destruction of a Chinese&nbsp;
base, but as they were getting ready to go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:37.120
Colonel Ling planned on shooting Captain&nbsp;
America in the back. And that's when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:42.160
Shostakov made a split-second decision to&nbsp;
sacrifice his own life to save Cap, Natasha,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:46.640
and their allies. The Red Guardian may have&nbsp;
been a supervillain, but he died like a hero.

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:52.800
The second Red Guardian — who first appeared&nbsp;
in The Defenders #35 — wore basically the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:58.240
same costume as the first, but was different in&nbsp;
every other way. First of all, she was a woman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:03.520
a Russian neurosurgeon named Dr. Tania Belinsky.&nbsp;
Secondly, she wasn't a government operative. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:08.240
fact, she used her costumed identity to protect&nbsp;
dissidents within the U.S.S.R., as well as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:11.920
fighting crime. That was a pretty dangerous&nbsp;
way to live, so when Doctor Strange brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:16.320
her to the United States to perform delicate&nbsp;
brain surgery, she decided to stick around.

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:20.400
As the Red Guardian, she became a member of&nbsp;
the Defenders and a member of the American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:24.560
superhero community. Although she disagreed&nbsp;
with the oppressive Soviet government,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:28.560
Tania was herself a communist, which&nbsp;
gave her a different perspective on all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:30.800
sorts of matters from the rest of the Defenders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:36.480
who were mostly American capitalists ... plus&nbsp;
one Asgardian Valkyrie and an Atlantean prince.

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:41.200
During her time with the Defenders, the&nbsp;
Red Guardian was targeted by a Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:45.280
agent who exposed Tania and himself&nbsp;
to a high dose of nuclear radiation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:50.560
which gave them both superpowers, but it also&nbsp;
made them highly radioactive. For a time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:54.880
she was under his complete mental control,&nbsp;
thanks to his new powers. But ultimately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:58.880
Tania helped the Defenders defeat&nbsp;
the agent, now known as the Presence.

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:03.040
Unfortunately, the level of radiation she was&nbsp;
giving off meant that the Red Guardian had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:07.360
isolate herself from humanity to keep from&nbsp;
killing everyone around her. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:11.840
her days as an active superhero were&nbsp;
over, and she had to leave the Defenders.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:14.960
She would return from time to time&nbsp;
over the years, and eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:19.280
she got her radiation under control and&nbsp;
operated under the name Starlight. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:23.840
she was never as prominent a character as&nbsp;
during her initial run with the Defenders.

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:30.160
The third Red Guardian first appeared in&nbsp;
Captain America #352. Named Josef Petkus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:35.120
this Guardian represented a return to the&nbsp;
original concept of a Soviet super-agent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:39.360
and direct counterpart to Captain&nbsp;
America. But by his introduction in 1989,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:42.800
relations between the United States and&nbsp;
the Soviet Union were much more civil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:46.960
and the relationship between Captain America&nbsp;
and the Red Guardian was likewise. There was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:52.480
tension and a bit of mistrust between the two&nbsp;
super-soldiers, but they were ultimately allies.

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:56.240
In fact, the story that introduced the&nbsp;
new Red Guardian had him taking Cap on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.240 --> 00:05:59.680
a walking tour of Moscow, and then they&nbsp;
fought a monster that turned out to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:04.160
the manifestation of the sublimated powers of&nbsp;
another group of forgotten Russian superbeings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:08.960
It was a very different sort of story that&nbsp;
perfectly represented the sometimes strained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:13.840
peace that existed between two of the world's&nbsp;
superpowers as the Cold War drew to a close.

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:20.000
In a certain sense, the Josef Petkus Red Guardian&nbsp;
split the difference between Alexei Shostakov and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:25.040
Tania Belinsky. Like Shostakov, Petkus worked for&nbsp;
the Soviet government and served as a mirror to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:29.760
Captain America, right down to carrying a shield&nbsp;
of his own, instead of a flying belt buckle.

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:33.040
However, like Belinsky, he was a&nbsp;
true hero of the Russian people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:38.000
not a brutish villain in a superhero's clothes.&nbsp;
When he and Cap were strolling through Moscow in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:42.320
his first appearance, the Guardian was cheered&nbsp;
and greeted affectionately by the public,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:46.960
who regarded him as a greater hero than his&nbsp;
American counterpart. He even lifted a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:52.080
girl who asked to "fly like a superhero"&nbsp;
and tossed the delighted child in the air.

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:56.320
This was a time, even in the&nbsp;
primary-colored world of superhero comics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.320 --> 00:07:01.120
when Americans were by and large ready to&nbsp;
accept that being a communist — even being a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:06.080
Soviet — didn't mean you were a bad person, and&nbsp;
the character of Josef Petkus reflected that.

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:14.000
On December 26, 1991, the Soviet Union collapsed&nbsp;
after years of unrest and loosening restrictions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:17.760
With no more Soviet government, there&nbsp;
could be no more Soviet operatives as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:21.200
villains or conflicted heroes in&nbsp;
comic books set in the present.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:24.960
Marvel Comics, with their 30-year&nbsp;
legacy of Soviet supervillains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:31.360
dealt with this by publishing a 1992 one-shot&nbsp;
comic aptly titled Soviet Super-Soldiers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:36.080
This title told the story of how the various&nbsp;
costumed Soviet characters in the Marvel Universe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:40.720
dealt with the collapse of the state that had&nbsp;
given so many of them their powers and identities.

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:45.440
Josef Petkus remained the Red Guardian,&nbsp;
and he eventually became part of a reformed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:49.200
super-team called the Winter Guard, which&nbsp;
kept the peace in Russia and around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:53.440
world in turbulent times. He eventually&nbsp;
changed his name to the Steel Guardian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:57.840
and he gave his life in the line of&nbsp;
duty, like the hero he'd always been.

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:02.800
For 24 years of Marvel Comics, Alexei&nbsp;
Shostakov was regarded as the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:06.560
Red Guardian. At some point, however,&nbsp;
it occurred to somebody at Marvel that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:12.000
both Captain America and the Soviet Union&nbsp;
had both existed for decades before 1967,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:15.520
so there was no reason not to have&nbsp;
an earlier version of the character.

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:21.200
So 1991's Namor, The Sub-Mariner&nbsp;
Annual #1 introduced Aleksey Lebedev,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:26.480
the Red Guardian of World War II. Naturally, he&nbsp;
was an ally of Captain America against the forces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:31.920
of Nazi Germany. But after the war, Lebedev&nbsp;
soon found himself opposing his former allies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:35.680
Although his full story has yet to be&nbsp;
told, he was said to have been killed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:39.840
by his own government during the 1950s,&nbsp;
revising the origin story of the Alexei&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:44.480
Shostakov Red Guardian to be an attempt to&nbsp;
create a more loyal replacement for Lebedev.

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:50.720
As the flagship superhero of Russia, the&nbsp;
Red Guardian often seems to be regarded by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:54.960
Marvel as more of a concept worth keeping&nbsp;
around than a fondly remembered character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:59.920
and thus, there have been more recent Guardians&nbsp;
who were never really developed beyond a costume.

00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:04.800
Krassno Granitsky, the fifth Red Guardian,&nbsp;
was introduced in Maverick Vol. 2 #10.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:08.000
After appearing in three issues of&nbsp;
that book, he was assassinated by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:12.400
a KGB general the very next time he&nbsp;
showed up, in Captain America Vol. 5 #1.

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:15.920
The sixth Red Guardian first&nbsp;
appeared in Hulk Vol. 2 #1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:20.320
and the only real name he was ever given&nbsp;
was Anton. A member of the Winter Guard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:25.840
he was by all accounts kind of a jerk,&nbsp;
and he also turned out to be an android.

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:29.200
The seventh and most recent Red&nbsp;
Guardian was Nicolai Krylenko,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:34.240
a Russian superman who was previously known as&nbsp;
Vanguard. He first showed up under that name in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:40.560
1978's Iron Man #109, but he didn't take on the&nbsp;
identity of the Red Guardian until Darkstar and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:47.920
the Winter Guard #3 in 2010 … after Anton the&nbsp;
android Red Guardian got his head ripped off.

